<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Panel \(a\) shows a phase diagram with regions labeled Homogeneous, Bistable, and Demixed based on variables \\\( \\alpha \\\) and \\\( p_0 \\\). Panel \(b\) presents a graph with three curves showing the relationship between \\\( p_{AB} \\\) and \\\( x \\\). Panel \(c\) depicts a curve illustrating \\\( I_h \\\) as a function of \\\( p_0 \\\). Panel \(d\) displays two curves and arrows indicating changes in \\\( I_h \\\) against \\\( p_0 \\\).)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
24 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Two plots showing the relationship between certain variables. Plot \(a\) graphs \\\(T_n\\\) versus \\\(p_0\\\) with three curves for \\\(r_s\\\) values: 0 \(red\), 0.1 \(blue\), and 0.2 \(green\). Plot \(b\) graphs \\\(T_n\\\) versus \\\(r_s\\\) for \\\(p_0\\\) values: 0.357 \(blue\), 0.37 \(orange\), and 0.4 \(green\). Each plot demonstrates threshold behavior with different curves.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 17 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
25 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Line graph showing the relationship between \\\( p_0 \\\) and \\\( I_h \\\) for different values of \\\( N \\\). Solid and dashed lines represent \\\( N = 10^4 \\\), \\\( 5 \\times 10^3 \\\), \\\( 10^3 \\\), and \\\( 5 \\times 10^2 \\\) with colors green, dark green, blue, and red, respectively. The lines show various increasing trends as \\\( p_0 \\\) ranges from 0.35 to 0.37.)
/K 1
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 17 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
26 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Graphical data visualization includes two parts: \(a\) time series plots showing \\\(I_h\\\) and \\\(\\langle x_A \\rangle\\\) in blue, red, and green lines for \\\(p_0 = 0.354\\\), \\\(p_0 = 0.357\\\), and \\\(p_0 = 0.36\\\) respectively. \(b\) Probability distribution plot of \\\(P\(I_h\)\\\) with three curves representing different \\\(p_0\\\) values, corresponding to the colors from part \(a\).)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 18 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
27 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Contour plot illustrating the relationship between \\\( P\(I_h\) \\\) and \\\( p_0 \\\). The graph features a blue line representing \\\( I_h \\\), with red and black dots indicating PDF maximum and minimum points respectively. Concentric curves show gradient variations, with red and black points highlighting specific data intersections. A color gradient transitions from blue and purple to green and yellow, indicating intensity changes. The plot is labeled with \\\( p_0 \\\) on the x-axis and \\\( P\(I_h\) \\\) on the y-axis.)
/K 0
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
28 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Line graph showing mean residence time on the y-axis and \\\(p_0\\\) on the x-axis. Three datasets are represented: red for \\\(N = 300\\\), blue for \\\(N = 500\\\), and green for \\\(N = 1000\\\). Data points are connected with lines showing trends in residence time for varying values of \\\(p_0\\\).)
/K 1
/P 7 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
>>
endobj
29 0 obj
<<
/A 249 0 R
/Alt (Graph \(a\) shows correlation time versus \\\( p_0 \\\) for \\\( N = 300, 500, \\\) and \\\( 1000 \\\). The curves peak around \\\( p_0 = 0.355 \\\). Graph \(b\) plots correlation time against \\\( N \\\) for \\\( p_0 = 0.357, 0.34, \\\) and \\\( 0.38 \\\). The red line for \\\( p_0 = 0.357 \\\) indicates a positive correlation, while the others are constant.)
